WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.360
Hey, what's up, everyone? My&nbsp;
name is Luv Aggarwal and I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.680
a Data Platform Solution Engineer for IBM.  
 
 

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.960
Data warehouses. Their prevalence across&nbsp;
enterprises has grown significantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:15.600
over the past 20+ years. But with&nbsp;
multiple modern advancements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.480
the numerous options out there&nbsp;
are now much more complex.  
 
 

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:25.920
So, let's talk about what an enterprise data&nbsp;
warehouse, or "EDW", is. So, first and foremost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:43.520
there's often confusion between "data lakes"&nbsp;
and "data warehouses" and even "data marts".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:52.080
So, I like to think of a data warehouse as being&nbsp;
more purpose-specific than a data lake. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:57.360
while a data lake is a great place to dump all&nbsp;
sorts of raw, structured and unstructured data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.560
in a quick way to clean and organize later, a&nbsp;
data warehouse, on the other hand, is a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:08.240
collection of organized and clean business data,&nbsp;
ready to help an organization make decisions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.960
And a data mart is like a subset of a&nbsp;
data warehouse that's more specific to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:19.040
particular business domain. So, for example,&nbsp;
you could have a finance data mart.  
 
 

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.240
But for today, let's focus on the data warehouse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:22.346
So, we'll get rid of data lakes and data marts,&nbsp;
and we'll make this a little bit bigger.  
 
 

00:01:22.346 --> 00:01:24.560
But for today, we'll focus on the data warehouse.&nbsp;
So, let's get rid of data lakes and data marts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.400
and make our data warehouse&nbsp;
a little bit bigger.  
 
 

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.720
So, the data warehouse serves as the single source&nbsp;
of truth for an organization across multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:41.280
knowledge domains. And data in the warehouse&nbsp;
comes from multiple different source systems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.600
And is transformed from raw&nbsp;
data to high quality data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:56.480
optimized for analytics via various different&nbsp;
ETL, or "Extract, Transform and Load" tools.  
 
 

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:04.400
So, as I mentioned, data that's&nbsp;
in our source systems can be in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:08.320
different types. It could be transactional&nbsp;
systems, it can be relational databases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:11.440
and they can cover a wide&nbsp;
variety of business domains.  
 
 

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:21.840
So, the data could cover things like customer&nbsp;
data from our CRMs. We could have sales data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:28.880
We could have data from our ERP systems.&nbsp;
We could even have supply chain data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.800
And the list goes on and on. Right.  
 
 

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.160
So, once data has been cleaned, transformed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:44.560
loaded into our data warehouse, it's&nbsp;
now ready for us to expose to our users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:51.600
who can then start to take it and do analytics&nbsp;
and machine learning on these data sets.  
 
 

00:02:52.320 --> 00:03:02.560
So, who are our users? Our users can be folks&nbsp;
like business analysts. We can have data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:16.240
scientists. We could even have data engineers. And&nbsp;
these folks can now start leveraging these data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:23.440
sets, either using the built-in analytics tools in&nbsp;
the data warehouse or using a variety of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:32.640
business intelligence or predictive&nbsp;
analytics and machine learning platforms.  
 
 

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:37.840
OK, so now that we know what an&nbsp;
enterprise data warehouse is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.520
let's talk about the different ways&nbsp;
in which it can be implemented.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:46.000
So, three common ways in which a&nbsp;
data warehouse can be deployed.  
 
 

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:52.800
The first way is on-premises. Now,&nbsp;
a couple different ways in which an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:58.240
on-prem data warehouse can be configured,&nbsp;
we could have our data warehouse running on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:08.880
commodity hardware. Now, this could be set up&nbsp;
and structured using either MPP, or "Massively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:14.720
Parallel Processing", architecture where we just&nbsp;
add more compute nodes as our workload grows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:23.520
or using SMP, or "Symmetric Multi-Processing",&nbsp;
architecture where, typically, we have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:29.440
tightly coupled, multi-CPU system that shares&nbsp;
resources from one common operating system.  
 
 

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:36.080
Now, the other way is through a&nbsp;
purpose-built appliance format.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:45.680
Now, this is typically an integrated&nbsp;
stack of CPU, memory storage software,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:50.720
all purpose-built and optimized for a data&nbsp;
warehouse workload from a single vendor.  
 
 

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:56.240
So, what are some of the benefits&nbsp;
of having an on-prem data warehouse?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:02.240
Well, first you get to maintain complete&nbsp;
control over the entire tech stack, right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:11.680
Second, you can leverage your local network&nbsp;
speeds and perhaps avoid some bandwidth challenges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:19.520
typically associated with the cloud. You can also&nbsp;
leverage high availability, and we can maintain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:27.760
strict governance and regulatory compliance, but&nbsp;
on the other hand, an on-prem data warehouse does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:32.400
come with an upfront investment and the&nbsp;
need for ongoing support and maintenance.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:36.960
Now, the other way in which a&nbsp;
data warehouse can be deployed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:43.040
is through a cloud-based data warehouse,&nbsp;
where our data warehouse is delivered as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:49.200
a managed to SaaS offering via the&nbsp;
multiple public cloud providers.  
 
 

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:56.640
So, moving data warehouses to the cloud is&nbsp;
the next frontier for a lot of enterprises&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:02.640
and for valid reasons. Some of the benefits&nbsp;
include being able to free up resources&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:09.440
to focus on other high value analytics tasks,&nbsp;
right, instead of just managing systems.  
 
 

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:15.840
Another benefit can also be the&nbsp;
ability to scale easily. Right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:18.160
because we don't have to go&nbsp;
out and procure new hardware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:31.600
and we get to leverage automatic upgrades. Right.&nbsp;
Now, on the other hand, oftentimes a cloud-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:37.040
data warehouse can take a performance hit due to&nbsp;
how it's fine tuned for that specific workload,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:43.040
and there can be some unanticipated high costs&nbsp;
due to how cloud data warehouse is scaled.  
 
 

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:54.480
OK, the third option is actually a hybrid&nbsp;
approach. So, this takes the best of on-prem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:59.440
and cloud and brings them together. And a lot&nbsp;
of enterprises choose to run both their on-prem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:05.680
and cloud data warehouses in conjunction. And this&nbsp;
can be done for a couple of different reasons.  
 
 

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:13.280
So, one benefit can be that this allows us to&nbsp;
explore new use-cases. Right. So as an enterprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:18.240
we may have certain data sources that&nbsp;
were born in the cloud. So, it can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:24.480
beneficial to start leveraging a cloud data&nbsp;
warehouse for analytics against those use-cases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:27.840
while still maintaining their mission&nbsp;
critical workloads on-prem.  
 
 

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:36.800
Another benefit can be for a disaster&nbsp;
recovery and backup scenario.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:44.080
This is where we would use both our environments&nbsp;
in conjunction for DR and backup reasons.  
 
 

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:49.440
So, if we take a step back, we can see that&nbsp;
we've barely started to scratch the surface of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:55.760
enterprise data warehouses and how they fit into&nbsp;
an overall enterprise architecture. But I hope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:02.880
this video has given us a good idea of how data&nbsp;
warehouses fit in and what they're used for. Thank&nbsp;&nbsp;

